# dcfs-specific pjdfstest failures (step 4.5): checks that fail against the
# dcfs mount but pass when the identical check is run directly against the
# backing ext4 filesystem (see guest/pjdfstest.sh, which computes this set
# fresh on every run and diffs it against this file). One
# "<relative .t path>:<TAP test number within that file>" per line, sorted;
# blank lines and "#"-comment lines are ignored.
#
# guest/pjdfstest.sh FAILS if any freshly observed dcfs-specific failure is
# NOT listed here (a regression), and prints an "info:" line for any entry
# below that no longer fails (so this file can be tightened). See
# docs/conformance.md for the full triage; in short, every entry below is
# one symptom of a single known, unfixed limitation: dcfs runs as root and
# performs every backing-filesystem write as root, with no per-request
# uid/gid (or supplementary-group) impersonation of the FUSE caller. A
# file/directory a non-root user creates through dcfs is therefore always
# owned by root on the backing filesystem instead of that user, so every
# later permission check that assumes the creator is the ows (chmod/chown/
# open/truncate/rename by the "owner", or denied to a non-owner who should
# be blocked) sees the wrong owner and gets the wrong answer. Fixing this
# properly (setfsuid(2)/setfsgid(2) impersonation, plus setgroups(2) for
# the multi-group cases, around every backing write) is a real feature,
# not a two-attempt bug fix, and out of scope for this step.
